<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Frederick, Duke of Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1553">1553</a>–<a href="/years/1618">1618</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Frederick (1553–1618) was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1568, the son and successor of Albert, the first duke. Mentally ill from his youth, he was unable to rule, and his Hohenzollern relatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> governed as regents. On his death the duchy passed to his son-in-law, Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-elector-of-brandenburg">John Sigismund</a>, creating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-frederick-duke-of-prussia.jpg" alt="Albert Frederick, Duke of Prussia" width="320" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Albert_Frederick,_Duke_of_Prussia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albert Frederick was born on 7 May 1553, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Prussia</a> and Anna Marie of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>. He was the second and last Prussian duke of the Ansbach branch of the Hohenzollern family. He became duke after paying feudal homage to his cousin, King Sigismund Augustus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a> on 19 July 1569. The Polish chronicler Jan Kochanowski described the homage in his work Proporzec.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As the great-grandson of the Polish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> and a duke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> who spoke fluent Polish, Albert Frederick was for a time seriously considered as a candidate for the Polish throne, with the support of Polish Lutherans. In 1573 he tried to gain acceptance to the Polish senate but was opposed by Jan Zamoyski, who feared the influence of Protestants in the legislature. He at first refused to recognise the election of Stefan Bathory and backed Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, but gave Bathory his support at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> sejm of October 1576. In 1573 he married Marie Eleonore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, daughter of Wilhelm, Duke of Jülich-Cleves-Berg.</p><p>From 1572 he showed signs of mental disorder. He twice tried to commit suicide, was prone to violent outbursts and held a great fear that Turks and Muscovites would overrun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In early 1578 his cousin George Frederick of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a> took over as regent, and after his death in 1603 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> appointed Joachim Frederick as regent in 1605.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Albert Frederick and Marie Eleonore had seven children, of whom two died in infancy. Their daughters married into the houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, among them Anna, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-elector-of-brandenburg">John Sigismund, Elector of Brandenburg</a>, and Eleanor, who married Joachim Frederick. Sigismund III allowed John Sigismund to succeed in 1611. Albert Frederick died on 27 August 1618, and the duchy passed to his son-in-law John Sigismund, uniting the two territories under one dynasty and forming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albert Frederick, Duke of Prussia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primorsk-kaliningrad-oblast">Primorsk</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.73,20&amp;year=1595" aria-label="Primorsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4990 291 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Albert Frederick, Duke of Prussia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5440 561h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5440 561h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=54.73,20&amp;year=1595">What was here in 1595? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
